Never:

- Commit API keys.
- Expose secrets.
- Hardcode user identities.
- Trust arbitrary user IDs from the frontend.
- Allow AI to bypass authorization.
- Expose private health records.
- Store sensitive credentials in plaintext.
- Claim security compliance without evidence.

### Rule 7 — Preserve medical safety

All medical-facing features must be cautious, transparent and evidence-aware.

The platform provides education and decision support, not a substitute for a healthcare professional.

### Rule 8 — Avoid unnecessary dependencies

Before installing a package:

- Check whether an equivalent package is already installed.
- Verify version compatibility.
- Explain why it is necessary.
- Avoid upgrading unrelated dependencies.

### Rule 9 — Do not delete files casually

A file that appears unused may still be required by a route, build, test or mobile component.

Search references before removing it.

### Rule 10 — Do not fabricate test results

Only report tests that were actually run.

Distinguish:

- Implemented.
- Partially implemented.
- Tested.
- Not tested.
- Blocked.
- Pending.

### Rule 11 — Maintain documentation

Update documentation when architecture, API contracts, database schemas or deployment processes change.

### Rule 12 — Maintain a progress ledger

After every completed phase, update:

- Completed tasks.
- Changed files.
- New database migrations.
- API changes.
- Tests executed.
- Test results.
- Known bugs.
- Outstanding tasks.
- Next recommended phase.

Never mark a phase complete based only on code generation.

